    pr_ref:  # bd9f
    description: "FOUND 2026-09-27 while building WI-gotun's DDG spec (java_callable_name had an unreachable-looking None branch; a record reaches it).\n\nREPRO, dev plus the WI-gotun branch:\n\n    record R(int a) {\n      int twice() { return a * 2; }\n      static R of(int a) { return new R(a); }\n    }\n\n`analyze_java` on a repo holding only that file returns [] -- no symbols at all: no `R` type, no `twice`, no `of`.\n\nMECHANISM (java.py). `_get_class_ancestors` walks up for `class_declaration`, `interface_declaration` and `enum_declaration`; `record_declaration` is not in the set. The method and constructor arms require `name and ancestors`, so a method whose only enclosing type is a record is dropped. The record type itself is not emitted by any arm (not verified which arm would own it). A record nested in a class gets its methods named on the OUTER class, skipping R (not verified).\n\nWHY IT MATTERS. Records are Java 16+ (2021) and are the idiomatic data carrier in modern Java: DTOs, config and value types. Every call inside a record method is lost from the call graph, taint and dead-code. The DDG (WI-gotun) mirrors the analyzer through the shared `java_callable_name`, so it skips record methods too; that is consistent, and both change together when this is fixed.\n\nNOT MEASURED: how many records the corpus holds (jenkins / cassandra / sherpa-onnx are likely pre-16, so the count there is probably low; a modern Spring Boot repo is the test). Check `compact_constructor_declaration` too, the record-only constructor form."  # bd9f
    fields: {}  # bd9f
